<body lang=3Den-US style=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>

<div style=3D'direction:ltr;margin-top:0in;margin-left:0in;width:6.5in'>
<div style=3D'direction:ltr;margin-top:0in;margin-left:0in;width:6.5in'>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:"Calibri Light";font-size:20.0pt' lang=
=3Den-US>Deployment runbook - Orders API</p>
</div>
<div style=3D'direction:ltr;margin-top:.0423in;margin-left:0in;width:1.6416=
in'>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-size:10.0pt;color:#767676'><span style=3D'font-=
family:Calibri'>Monday, March 3, 2025</span></p>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-size:10.0pt;color:#767676'><span style=3D'font-=
family:Calibri'>9:30 AM</span></p>
</div>
<div style=3D'direction:ltr;margin-top:.4in;margin-left:0in;width:6.5in'>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>This page desc=
ribes how we deploy the Orders API to the staging and production environmen=
ts. The service runs on Azure App Service with two deployment slots, stagin=
g and production, and we swap slots instead of deploying straight to produc=
tion.</p>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Before you sta=
rt, make sure the release branch has passed the full CI pipeline, including=
 the integration tests against the shared test database. If any test is fla=
ky, rerun it once. If it fails twice, stop and raise it in the team channel=
.</p>
<ul type=3Ddisc style=3D'direction:ltr;unicode-bidi:embed;margin-top:0in;ma=
rgin-bottom:0in'>
<li style=3D'margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;vertical-align:middle'><span styl=
e=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Check the release notes are writ=
ten and linked from the ticket.</span></li>
<li style=3D'margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;vertical-align:middle'><span styl=
e=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Confirm the database migration s=
cript has been reviewed by a second developer.</span></li>
<li style=3D'margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;vertical-align:middle'><span styl=
e=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Tell the support team the planne=
d deployment window at least one day ahead.</span></li>
<li style=3D'margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;vertical-align:middle'><span styl=
e=3D'font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Make sure someone from the on-ca=
ll rota is available during the deployment.</span></li>
</ul>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Step one is to=
 run the database migration against staging. The migrations are written to =
be backwards compatible, which means the old version of the code must still=
 work after the migration runs. We never drop or rename a column in the sam=
e release that stops using it. Instead we stop using it first, then remove =
it in a later release once we are sure nothing reads it anymore.</p>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Step two is to=
 deploy the build artifact to the staging slot. Use the release pipeline in=
 Azure DevOps and pick the build number from the CI run you checked earlier=
. Do not rebuild from source at this point, because a rebuild can pick up a=
 different version of a dependency and you would be deploying something nob=
ody tested.</p>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Step three is =
the smoke test. Open the staging URL and run the smoke test collection. It =
creates a test order, reads it back, updates the delivery address and cance=
ls it. All four calls should return success within two seconds. Also check =
Application Insights for any new exceptions in the last fifteen minutes.</p>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Step four is t=
he slot swap. In the Azure portal, open the App Service, go to Deployment s=
lots and choose Swap. Source is staging, target is production. The swap war=
ms up the staging instances before routing traffic, so users should not not=
ice anything. Watch the live metrics for at least ten minutes after the swa=
p finishes.</p>
<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt'>Rollback is th=
e same operation in reverse: swap the slots again. Because the old version =
is still sitting in the staging slot after a swap, rolling back takes about=
 a minute. This is the main reason we use slots. The database migration doe=
s not need to be rolled back because it is backwards compatible, as describ=
ed above.</p>
